  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon web app\n\nThis is a Next.js (App Router) console with Drizzle/Postgres, almost entirely server-rendered. The codebase is unusually disciplined about query shape already — most files have comments explicitly reasoning about batching (`countChildrenByParent`, `Promise.all` groupings, single joined queries). Below is what actually turned up, organized by your five categories.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found\n\nI read through `lib/server/{conflicts,runs,rulesets,sources,entities,export,audit}.ts`. Every list view that could have gone row-by-row instead batches:\n\n- `getQueuePage` (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:172`) does rows + counts + total in one `Promise.all`, then a **single** grouped query (`countChildrenByParent`) for child counts across the whole page instead of one query per row.\n- `buildRunExport` (`lib/server/export.ts:122`) and `loadOrphans` (`lib/server/export.ts:236`) follow the same pattern.\n\nOne thing worth fixing for correctness (which would *become* a silent double-counting bug, not classic N+1): `lib/server/conflicts.ts:61-66` calls out that `resolutions.conflict_id` has no unique index enforcing \"one resolution per conflict.\" If that invariant is ever violated, every `leftJoin(resolutions, ...)` in that file fans a conflict out into duplicate rows and the counts double. Worth adding:\n```sql\ncreate unique index resolutions_conflict_id_key on resolutions (conflict_id);\n```\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — the queue is the real target\n\nThe conflict queue (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`, `conflict-dense-table.tsx`, `conflict-diff-row.tsx`, `conflict-focus.tsx`) can hold **100 rows** (`QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100`, `types/queue.ts:134`) and is driven entirely by keyboard (`j`/`k` to move selection). Every keystroke updates `selectedIndex` in the parent, and since none of the row components are memoized, **all 100 rows re-render** on every navigation, even though only the previously-selected and newly-selected row actually change visually.\n\nIt's compounded by inline closures recreated per row per render, e.g. in `conflict-queue.tsx:174-186`:\n```tsx\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    ...\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}                 // new fn every render\n    onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)}  // new fn every render\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\nEven if you wrap `ConflictDiffRow` in `React.memo`, these props defeat it because they're new references every render.\n\n**Fix** — memoize the row components and make their callback props stable by keying on id instead of closing over it fresh each render:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ ... }) {\n  ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — pass stable, id-parameterized callbacks instead of\n// creating a closure per row\nconst handleSelect = useCallback((index: number) => setSelectedIndex(index), []);\nconst handleOverrideToggle = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string, open: boolean) => setOverrideConflictId(open ? conflictId : null),\n  [],\n);\n\n// row:\n<ConflictDiffRow\n  onSelect={() => handleSelect(index)}   // still recreated, but handleSelect itself is stable\n  onOverrideOpenChange={handleOverrideToggle}   // pass row.conflictId down instead of pre-binding\n  conflictId={row.conflictId}\n/>\n```\nSame issue applies to `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow` list (`conflict-focus.tsx:57-66`) — wrap `ListRow` in `React.memo` too; it's the one rendering 100 nav items on every arrow key.\n\nGiven rows are ~150-200px of markup each with several nested `cn()` calls, memoizing these two components is the single highest-value React fix here — it turns \"100 rows re-render\" into \"2 rows re-render\" per keystroke.\n\n### 3. Redundant computations\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61,167`:\n```tsx\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);   // full sort, every render, unmemoized\n...\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);             // O(n) lookup inside an O(n) map → O(n²)\n  ...\n```\nSmall `n` today (a handful of rules), so it's not costing anything yet, but it's a needless O(n²) pass and an unmemoized sort on a component that re-renders on every keystroke in any field. Fix:\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(\n  () => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules).map((rule) => ({ rule, index: rules.indexOf(rule) })),\n  [rules],\n);\n```\n\nEverything else I checked (`toQueueRow`, `applyDecisions`, CSV building in `export.ts`) is a single linear pass over already-fetched data — not redundant.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nOnly three client components use `useEffect`/timers/listeners, and all clean up correctly:\n- `run-live.tsx`'s `Elapsed` ticker (`setInterval` + `clearInterval` in the effect return)\n- `conflict-queue.tsx`'s `keydown` listener (`addEventListener` + `removeEventListener`)\n- `conflict-focus.tsx`'s `ListRow` scroll-into-view effect (no subscription, nothing to leak)\n\nTanStack Query's polling (`run-live.tsx:46-60`) stops itself once `isTerminalRunStatus` is true, and the `QueryProvider` is mounted at the one leaf that needs it rather than the root, per `components/query-provider.tsx`'s own comment — so it doesn't accumulate client-side cache across the rest of the app.\n\n### 5. Caching opportunities\n\nThe app deliberately has **no client-side data cache** outside the one polling screen (`AGENTS.md` intent, confirmed in `query-provider.tsx`). That's a real architectural choice, not an oversight — most screens are Server Components reading Postgres directly on every request, which is simpler to reason about but means low-churn reference data gets refetched every navigation:\n\n- `listSources()` — hit on `/sources`, `/rules`, `/runs/new`, and `POST /api/sources` (`lib/server/sources.ts:14`)\n- `listRulesets()` / `getActiveRuleset()` — hit on `/rules`, `/runs/new` (`lib/server/rulesets.ts:19,31`)\n\nThese change rarely (a source is added occasionally; a ruleset version is saved occasionally) but are re-queried from Postgres on every page load. Since `connection()` already opts these functions into per-request dynamic rendering (intentional, per the comment in `sources.ts:15-19`), the fix isn't to cache the *page* — it's to cache the *query* with tag-based invalidation, so navigations stay dynamic but skip the round-trip when nothing changed:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { unstable_cache, revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources-list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n\nexport async function createSource(input: CreateSource): Promise<Source> {\n  const [row] = await getDb().insert(sources).values(input).returning();\n  revalidateTag(\"sources\");\n  return sourceSchema.parse(row);\n}\n```\nSame pattern for `listRulesets`/`getActiveRuleset`, invalidated in `createRulesetVersion`. This is a small win given likely table sizes, but it's free correctness-wise (writes still invalidate immediately) and removes a DB round trip from every console navigation.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line**: the backend query layer is already close to best-practice for this shape of app — I'd leave it alone apart from the missing unique index. The one substantive, worth-doing change is memoizing the conflict-queue row components; that's the only place a user would actually feel jank (100-row re-render on every `j`/`k` press). The ruleset-editor and reference-data caching items are minor, low-risk cleanups rather than urgent fixes.\n",
